## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## branch_improve_loop  (v2 — one agent reviews+improves the branch
##                       diff in its natural flow, minimal framing)
##
## THE MISSION is a REVIEW campaign scoped to the BRANCH DIFF: review and
## improve the changes THIS branch introduces over `base_ref` (default
## "main") — the diff `git diff $(git merge-base <base_ref> HEAD)` measured
## against the WORKING TREE (so a prior pass's uncommitted fixes stay
## visible; the commit-only `base_ref...HEAD` form would hide them and the
## loop could never converge). It finds the REAL issues the change introduces
## or leaves — bugs, regressions, missing/weak tests, unhandled errors,
## quality problems IN THE DIFF — and improves them, committing each fix in
## stride. It does NOT re-litigate code the branch didn't touch.
##
## Sibling of `whole_improve_loop` v2: SAME shape (one adaptive `campaign`
## agent + a deterministic build/test gate + a bounded continuation loop +
## git-as-state + the opt-in PR path), DIFFERENT scope — whole_improve_loop
## applies ONE determined axis across the whole codebase; branch_improve_loop
## reviews+improves the diff of ONE branch. `whole_improve_loop`'s AXIS is
## replaced here by the branch diff itself: `base_ref` + the diff define the
## work.
##
## WHY THIS SHAPE. The v1 branch loop (ADR-011 chunking → ADR-052 mono/dual)
## was ~11 nodes of chunked cross-family review machinery
## (plan_chunks → alt → reviewer_claude/reviewer_gpt → streak_check →
## fix_claude/fix_gpt → prepare_commit → commit_changes). It OVER-FRAMED the
## work — the same lesson whole_improve_loop learned (ADR-058,
## docs/references/productive-session-patterns.md): "the deficit is framing,
## not capability"; "once framed, a campaign runs itself". A capable agent
## reviews a branch diff and fixes what it finds better as ONE flow — a living
## todo list, one verified commit per fix, in stride — than as an assembly
## line of chunk/review/fix/commit nodes.
##
## GRAPH (see the workflow block):
##   campaign     — ONE adaptive agent (claude_code, full tools). Mission =
##                  review + improve the branch diff, with a standing-autonomy
##                  contract that reproduces the productive-session shape
##                  AGENT-SIDE: read the branch diff, build a LIVING todo of
##                  the concrete real issues the change introduces or leaves,
##                  then the repeated unit locate → smallest fix → build →
##                  test → semantic COMMIT, one issue at a time, committing
##                  each fix AS it finishes. git IS the durable state — a
##                  re-dispatch re-runs `campaign`, which reads `git log` +
##                  re-diffs the branch and continues. No worklist/cursor
##                  scratch file. Termination output: {branch_clean,
##                  commits_this_pass, issues_remaining, needs_human, …}.
##   verify_build — adaptive, stack-agnostic: reads the `verify-build` skill
##     + verify_run  and writes the repo's real build+test into an OUT-OF-TREE
##                  <scratch_dir>/verify.sh; a DETERMINISTIC tool node
##                  re-runs it and gates on the REAL exit code (no LLM
##                  judgment). The tight real-feedback loop AND the
##                  anti-Goodhart truth oracle: the agent can't self-certify.
##   gate         — deterministic compute: green AND branch_clean → converged
##                  → ship; else loop back to `campaign` (RED → with the
##                  failure log so the agent fixes what it broke; green but
##                  issues remain → another pass). No LLM, no shell.
##   mr_gate      — deterministic: open the optional PR (the series of
##     + finalize_mr per-pass commits) when open_mr, else finish.
##
## CONTINUATION IS BOUNDED. The single declared loop
## (campaign→verify_build→verify_run→gate→campaign) is capped by
## `max_passes`; on exhaustion it ships what is banked. `iterion validate`
## reports NO undeclared cycle. LANDS CONTINUOUSLY: the agent commits each
## fix in stride, so an interrupted / budget-capped run has banked every
## committed fix (git is the state).
##
## RIGHT-ARTIFACT (docs/workflow_authoring_pitfalls.md): the campaign runs
## `git add -N .` BEFORE diffing so a branch that ADDS files shows those files
## in `git diff` (untracked files are otherwise invisible to `git diff`), and
## commits the UNCOMMITTED working tree AFTER its own build+test passes,
## staging untracked files (`git add -A`) so a fix that adds files actually
## lands; the deterministic verify gate then re-checks the committed tree.
##
## STACK/REPO-AGNOSTIC (CLAUDE.md "Catalog bots are repo-agnostic" +
## "Universal code bots"): the base_ref + the diff define the work. No
## language / package-manager literal, and no iterion-specific target path,
## appears in any var default, command body, or schema — campaign /
## verify_build are adaptive agents that read whatever repo they are pointed
## at.
##
## RETIRED FROM v1 (git history has the design): plan_chunks (+ the chunking
## dials), the `alt` condition router, reviewer_claude / reviewer_gpt, the
## streak_check convergence compute, fix_claude / fix_gpt, prepare_commit /
## commit_changes, and the review_mode / mono_family topology vars. The
## campaign self-reviews via a fresh re-review of the diff + the deterministic
## build/test gate + its own judgment (the operator's proven manual pattern),
## and commits each fix in stride — so the ADR-052 mono/dual review topology no
## longer applies to this bot (no reviewer nodes); it stays on the other
## review-loop bots. 11 nodes → 6.
## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

vars:
  workspace_dir: string = "${PROJECT_DIR}"

  ## Branch comparison base. The campaign reviews the diff
  ## `git diff $(git merge-base {{vars.base_ref}} HEAD)` — the changes
  ## introduced by the current branch on top of `base_ref`, measured against
  ## the WORKING TREE so a prior pass's uncommitted fixes are included (the
  ## commit-only `...HEAD` form would hide them and break convergence).
  ## Default "main" covers the GitHub/GitLab/Forgejo conventions; pass
  ## `--var base_ref=develop` (or any local ref) when reviewing against a
  ## different integration branch.
  base_ref: string = "main"

  ## Free-form extra context for the campaign agent (e.g. what the branch is
  ## supposed to do, known caveats). Empty = the diff speaks for itself.
  scope_notes: string = ""

  ## Out-of-tree, sandbox-writable scratch for the deterministic gate's
  ## verify script/log ONLY (no chunk files / worklist any more — git is the
  ## state). The engine resolves ${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR} to
  ## ~/.iterion/projects/<key>/scratch non-sandboxed and to the
  ## container-writable /tmp/iterion-scratch under a sandbox (never hardcode
  ## ~/.iterion here — the -full image's pinned non-host User cannot write a
  ## host-owned path → EACCES). Keeping it OUT of the target worktree is the
  ## repo-agnostic rule: never pollute someone else's tree.
  scratch_dir: string = "${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR}/branch-improve-loop"

  ## BASELINE (G5 — docs/references/productive-session-patterns.md). Known
  ## pre-existing test failures / flaky tests the campaign must SKIP rather
  ## than burn budget proving they aren't the branch's own. Empty (default) =
  ## the campaign establishes it once cheaply (a `git stash`-based check
  ## against base_ref) then skips what predates the branch. The
  ## dispatcher/operator can pre-fill it.
  baseline: string = ""

  ## Hard cap on continuation PASSES — the convergence backstop. Each pass is
  ## one campaign→verify cycle; the run stops (shipping what is banked) when
  ## the branch is reported clean + the tree is green, or when this cap is
  ## hit. Sizes the declared continuation loop. Raise it (with the budget) for
  ## a large branch diff; the agent commits in stride, so every pass banks
  ## real, landed work.
  ## NOTE loop semantics: the DSL `as continuation_loop(N)` bounds the
  ## loop-BACKS (re-entries), so the TOTAL number of passes is N+1 —
  ## max_passes: 2 yields up to 3 campaign→verify cycles (observed live,
  ## dogfood 019f3d4d-1aed).
  max_passes: int = 8

  ## ─── PR finalization (opt-in) ───────────────────────────────
  ## When open_mr=true, after convergence finalize_mr pushes the run's HEAD
  ## (the SERIES of per-pass commits) to the forge and opens a pull
  ## request (PR; merge request on GitLab). Off by default so a plain
  ## local run just leaves the commits on
  ## the operator's branch. The comment→PR trigger sets this true. This bot
  ## runs in a per-run git worktree (worktree: auto), so the commits advance
  ## the worktree HEAD and finalize_mr pushes that branch. Requires a
  ## forge_token secret (or host-authenticated gh/glab).
  open_mr: bool = false
  ## Branch name to push + open the PR from. Empty = derive a stable name
  ## from the run (iterion/improve/<run-id>).
  mr_branch: string = ""
  ## Integration base the PR targets. Empty = vars.base_ref.
  mr_base: string = ""
  ## Optional back-link target: the source issue this run answers, as
  ## "native:<id>" (local board) or a forge issue URL. When set, finalize_mr
  ## posts the opened PR's URL back as a comment on it.
  source_issue_ref: string = ""
  ## PR-context push-back: the existing forge branch the run's commits belong
  ## to (the PR's source branch). When set with open_mr=false and the campaign
  ## banked commits, the deterministic push_back_tool pushes HEAD to it — the
  ## PR-webhook trigger sets this so Billy's fixes land ON the PR instead of
  ## dying with the ephemeral cloud-runner worktree. Empty (default) keeps
  ## today's behaviour (commits stay on the local storage branch).
  push_branch: string = ""
  ## The PR Billy is hardening, as a forge URL
  ## (https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/pull/<n>). When set, publish_verdict
  ## posts Billy's review verdict as a comment ON that PR so the author sees
  ## the conclusion (what was corrected, or "reviewed clean") in the forge, not
  ## only in the iterion run. The PR-webhook trigger sets it. Empty = skip.
  pr_url: string = ""

  ## Prior review to START FROM, not re-derive. When Billy is invoked with
  ## `/billy` on a PR that Revi (review-pr) already reviewed, the iterion
  ## webhook handler seeds this with Revi's most recent findings for that PR
  ## (severity/category/file:line/detail per finding). Treat them as a
  ## high-signal head start — verify each against the current diff, FIX the
  ## real ones, and keep reviewing for anything Revi missed (Revi is
  ## read-only; you fix + commit). Empty (default) = no prior review, review
  ## the diff from scratch.
  prior_review: string = ""

  ## WHERE THE HUMAN SITS IN THE LOOP.
  ##   "end"    (default) — run to completion, the human reviews the result.
  ##   "middle" — after reading the diff and the prior review, and BEFORE any
  ##              invasive edit, present the plan (which findings you intend to
  ##              fix, which you intend to contest and why, what else you found)
  ##              and WAIT for the operator to arbitrate.
  ##
  ## Both are first-class. A reviewer alone already puts the human in the middle
  ## — findings land, the human decides — and this bot is the more invasive
  ## option, so it must not be the only shape on offer: "middle" is how someone
  ## keeps the arbitration while still delegating the work.
  pilot: string [enum: "end", "middle"] = "end"

  ## MERGE GATE. When a pull request is in play, the deterministic
  ## publish_verdict step posts a commit status on the PR head so a required
  ## check tracks the revision THIS run produced — a push moves the head, and a
  ## status posted on the old one leaves the required check absent, which blocks
  ## the PR for good with nothing pointing at why.
  ##
  ## gate_context is the check NAME. A required check applies to every pull
  ## request, so a repo where several bots gate different PRs gives them ONE
  ## shared context, pinned per repo on the integration launch_vars — the same
  ## value on every bot that can gate. See docs/merge-gate.md.
  gate_enabled: bool = true
  gate_context: string = "revi/review"

  ## Deterministic forge publish grant — INJECTED BY THE ITERION SERVER at
  ## launch when the run's team has a forge connection covering pr_url's repo.
  ## The run itself never holds a posting credential.
  forge_publish_url: string = ""
  forge_publish_token: string = ""

  ## ─── Plan phase ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  ## The plan phase itself — the AUTHORED triage plan the campaign fixes
  ## from — runs by default, on every deployment, whatever `plan_review`
  ## resolves to. off = skip planning altogether: the campaign starts
  ## immediately and plans in stride (the deliberate cost-saving posture
  ## for a small, well-scoped diff). Independent of `plan_review`, which
  ## gates ONLY the cross-model peer review of that plan. The plan-phase
  ## budget guard below bounds the phase either way.
  plan_phase: string [enum: "on", "off"] = "on"
  ## ─── Cross-model pair review of the plan ──────────────────────
  ## Gates ONLY the peer review — never the plan phase (plan_phase).
  ## Resolved at LAUNCH by pkg/reviewtopology from the run's
  ## credentials: auto → "on" iff a SECOND model family is credentialed
  ## (host detection locally, the sealed bundle on cloud), else "off" —
  ## the plan is then authored and handed to the campaign unreviewed,
  ## stamped as such, at zero extra cost. "on" forces the review (the
  ## peer reviewer then fails loudly on a missing credential).
  plan_review: string = "auto"
  ## What a MID-RUN peer failure (forfait window shut, provider down,
  ## credential revoked) does: "skip" (default) arms the reviewer's
  ## `action: skip` fallback route — the plan proceeds unreviewed,
  ## LOUDLY (_skipped stamped, plan_gate reads it). The peer is an
  ## optional enrichment: the Anthropic family alone must always
  ## suffice to fix a branch, so a dead peer never blocks the campaign
  ## (a fixer invoked on a PR that parks on an OPTIONAL reviewer holds
  ## the PR hostage). "wait" parks the run failed_resumable until the
  ## peer window reopens (run-level usage-window retry; cloud durable,
  ## local --auto-resume) — the deliberate-spend posture.
  plan_review_policy: string [enum: "wait", "skip"] = "skip"

  ## Plan-phase budget guard (native:695). A production run on a
  ## +3870/-273 diff spent the planning chain (plan -> plan_review ->
  ## plan_revise) for 150 min / $8.59 and never reached `campaign` — the
  ## whole run's budget died on the enrichment before a single commit.
  ## plan_budget_ratio caps the FRACTION of the run's own ceilings the
  ## planning chain may spend; plan_budget_gate (after the chain) fails
  ## the run before campaign starts the moment planning crosses its
  ## share, guaranteeing campaign at least (1-ratio) of the budget. The
  ## ceilings are read from the `run` namespace (run.max_duration_seconds
  ## / run.max_cost_usd), so they are the caps IN FORCE for this run --
  ## after any --max-duration / --max-cost-usd override, the recipe, the
  ## platform ceiling and any live raise_budget -- not a literal that
  ## drifts from the budget: block. Overridable per run
  ## (--var plan_budget_ratio=0.5). See docs/bot-runs/branch-improve-loop.md.
  plan_budget_ratio: float = 0.3

  ## Above this many added lines in the branch diff (git diff --shortstat
  ## insertions), the plan phase reads a git diff --stat footprint +
  ## per-file hunks instead of the full unified diff, and the
  ## author-revise turn (plan_revise) is skipped -- the peer's critique
  ## reaches the campaign UNREVISED rather than paying a second full-diff
  ## read. Both cuts keep the plan phase itself inside plan_budget_ratio
  ## on a large PR (repo-agnostic: pure line-count over git diff, no
  ## language/package-manager assumption).
  plan_large_diff_lines: int = 1500

secrets:
  ## Forge access for push + PR creation + the issue back-link. Mounted as a
  ## read-only file the gh/glab CLI authenticates from; never read into a
  ## prompt. optional:true so manual/local runs relying on host-authenticated
  ## gh/glab still work. See skills/forge-mr-create.md.
  forge_token:
    as: file
    optional: true

## ─── Prompts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

prompt campaign_system:
  You are a senior engineer running a focused REVIEW-AND-IMPROVE campaign on
  ONE branch's diff — working EXACTLY as you would in a productive,
  human-driven engineering session. You have the repo in front of you and full
  tools (read, edit, shell, git). No one micro-manages you; you own this pass
  end to end.

  THE SCOPE is the BRANCH DIFF, not the whole codebase. You are reviewing and
  improving the changes THIS branch introduces over `base_ref` (in your user
  prompt). FIRST read the diff — run `git add -N .` (intent-to-add, so any NEW
  files the branch adds show up) then
  `git diff $(git merge-base <base_ref> HEAD)` (merge-base vs WORKING TREE, so
  your OWN uncommitted fixes stay visible; the commit-only `<base_ref>...HEAD`
  form would hide them and the loop could never converge). Prepend
  `-c safe.directory='*'` if git reports "dubious ownership". Enumerate the
  branch footprint with
  `git diff $(git merge-base <base_ref> HEAD) --name-only`. THAT diff is your
  scope — do NOT re-litigate code the branch did not touch (you MAY read the
  surrounding code, callers, conventions ONLY to judge how the branch changes
  integrate).

  HOW TO WORK — reproduce the shape of a productive session, agent-side:

  1. LIVING TODO LIST, not frozen phases. Read the branch diff and build a
     LIVING todo of the CONCRETE, REAL issues the change introduces or leaves
     — bugs, regressions, missing or weak tests, unhandled errors, race
     conditions, security holes, quality problems IN THE DIFF. Keep it DENSE
     and MOVING — re-prioritize, append, and defer items as you learn. Do NOT
     write 5–6 coarse upfront phases and tick them: the list is a metronome
     born from reading the diff, not a plan frozen before it. Cover the
     production-ready axes AS THEY APPLY TO THE DIFF: security (auth, input
     validation, injection, path traversal, secret leaks), robustness (error
     handling, panics, leaked goroutines, missing timeouts, retries without
     backoff), data (migrations, transactions, idempotence, data loss),
     concurrency (unbalanced mutexes, double-close, atomicity), observability,
     performance (O(n^2) on hot paths, N+1), boundaries (recovery, corrupted
     input). A real blocker is a bug/regression/vulnerability/obvious dead
     code — NOT a style preference, naming, or a missing doc.

  2. THE REPEATED UNIT, one issue at a time: locate the exact site in the diff
     → make the SMALLEST fix that resolves the issue there → build → run the
     tests → COMMIT it with a semantic message (`fix(scope): <precise issue>`,
     or the `type(scope):` that fits — `test(...)` for an added test,
     `refactor(...)` for a quality fix, etc.). A few edits per commit,
     validation BEFORE the commit — never commit code you have not built and
     tested green.

  3. COMMIT EACH FIX AS YOU FINISH IT — never batch. Stage everything
     including new files (`git add -A`, so a fix that ADDS a test/helper
     actually lands) and commit before moving on. git IS your durable state:
     an interrupted run keeps every fix you committed, and a fresh pass just
     reads `git log`, re-diffs the branch, and continues where you left off.
     Do NOT keep a separate worklist / cursor file — the todo list lives in
     your working memory, the commits are the record.
     END EVERY PASS ON A CLEAN TREE: if you must stop mid-fix, either
     finish it to committable or discard the in-progress edits — `git
     restore --staged --worktree -- <the paths you touched>` and
     delete any new files you created; never a blanket `git clean`
     (the workspace may be the operator's own checkout). The
     deterministic gate verifies the WORKING TREE, and a half-done fix
     (an un-vendored dependency, a dangling import) reds the gate as
     noise.

  4. BASELINE — don't chase failures the branch didn't cause. Pre-existing
     test failures / known-flaky tests are in your user prompt (`baseline`).
     Do NOT try to fix or investigate those; if a test was already red on
     `base_ref` before this branch, SKIP it and note it. If the baseline is
     empty, do ONE cheap `git stash`-based check (or a run against base_ref)
     to establish which failures pre-date the branch, then stop investigating
     them. Don't burn budget proving a failure isn't the branch's.

  5. ASK ONLY ON A REAL DECISION. You MAY pause for the operator (the
     ask-user tool) on a genuine decision you cannot make on your own — is a
     behaviour intentional, a destructive trade-off, a backward-compat policy
     call. Keep it RARE: most of the time you decide and keep moving.

  6. PILOTING — read the `pilot` value in your task below.
     - "end": the operator reviews your RESULT. Work through, as above.
     - "middle": the operator arbitrates the PLAN. Read the diff and the prior
       review, decide what you WOULD do, then — BEFORE editing anything — ask
       the operator once, with: each prior finding and the outcome you propose
       for it (fix / contest, with your reason), anything else you found worth
       fixing, and anything you are unsure about. Then follow their answer:
       it overrides your own judgement on any point it covers, and you decide
       the rest yourself. Ask ONCE — this is an arbitration, not a dialogue.

  KEEP GOING, one issue at a time, until this pass has resolved every real
  issue you can find in the branch diff within your budget. Then STOP and
  report.

  BEFORE YOU REPORT, sharpen what you ship with three questions (advisory —
  the build/test gate still decides; this only improves your pass, it never
  blocks it):
    - Fit: does each fix resolve the real problem the issue named, or does it
      only make the symptom go away? A change that passes the gate while the
      underlying defect survives is a façade — redo it, don't report it fixed.
    - Rot: did your own fixes leave duplication, a one-caller abstraction, or
      a second way to do what the branch already does? Collapse it now, within
      the branch's own footprint — leave the diff more coherent than you found
      it, and never widen it to refactor code the branch did not touch.
    - Ratchet: for an issue you had to fix, what CHEAP deterministic check
      would have caught it — a regression test or an assertion AT THAT SITE,
      landed in a target the repo's own build already runs? If one is that
      small, land it: in the same commit while you are still on that issue,
      otherwise as its own follow-up commit. NEVER amend or rebase a commit
      you already landed — banked commits are the run's durable state and the
      branch may already be pushed. A check that can fail on code THIS BRANCH
      DID NOT TOUCH (a new lint rule, a stricter build-time constraint, any
      repo-wide gate) is not local: it turns the deterministic gate red on
      work that isn't the branch's. Name it in your summary, never land it —
      and the same for one that is simply too big. A check you declined is not
      itself a remaining issue: keep it out of issues_remaining. But a fix you
      shipped with NO test at all is still a "missing test" issue under the
      rubric above — that one stays. A check landed by an EARLIER PASS OF THIS
      RUN is already answered (its commit message names the issue it closes,
      and `git log` is the record): never re-open it as a "weak test" finding.
      A test that came WITH the branch is fair game. Under pilot "middle", a
      ratchet you already intend to land belongs in the plan you submit; one
      you only see after the arbitration goes in your summary, not in a second
      ask. This question sharpens what you ship; it never grows the pass,
      never blocks it.

  PERSISTENCE — the durable path, to the end. Never declare a
  sub-problem infeasible on reasoning alone: instrument it (add debug
  output, shrink the repro, bisect) and let the evidence decide — most
  premature verdicts of infeasibility are one experiment away from a
  small fix. Prefer the durable path over the expedient shortcut: no
  façade that satisfies the letter of the gate, no silently skipped or
  weakened test, no temporary patch where the real fix is reachable
  this pass. If something genuinely stays out of reach, bank your
  state first — commit what is green, then a precise `issues_remaining`
  note naming the paths you tried and the evidence that closed them —
  never a silent abandon.

  TERMINATION CONTRACT (required structured output — this is how the engine
  detects completion; an under-specified end just gets re-poked):
    - branch_clean: true ONLY when a fresh RE-REVIEW of the branch diff (re-run
      `git diff $(git merge-base <base_ref> HEAD)`) finds NO remaining real
      issue. false if any real issue remains (a later pass continues from your
      commits).
    - commits_this_pass: how many commits you landed this pass.
    - issues_remaining: a concise note of what real issues still need work
      (empty when branch_clean).
    - needs_human / human_note: set if you paused or need an operator decision.
    - summary: what you reviewed and what you improved, and how it relates to
      the branch's changes.
    - finding_ledger: ONE entry per finding of the PRIOR REVIEW (empty array
      when none was handed to you), keyed by the id it was shown under:
        {"id": "R7a3f", "status": "fixed", "commit": "<sha>", "note": ""}
        {"id": "R1b2c", "status": "refused", "note": "<why it is not a real
          problem — the evidence you checked>"}
        {"id": "R9e40", "status": "deferred", "note": "<what it needs>"}
      Account for EVERY id you were given: the author was shown those findings
      and is owed an answer on each. Silence on one reads as "ignored".
      A `refused` note is an ARGUMENT, checked by a human and fed to the next
      review — "not a real issue" alone is not one. State what you read and why
      it does not hold. Refusing is legitimate and sometimes correct; refusing
      without evidence is not, and it does NOT unblock anything: an unfixed
      finding keeps the merge gate red either way, so a refusal costs you
      nothing to argue honestly.

  Be honest. A DETERMINISTIC build/test gate re-checks the tree after you and
  the run loops you back for another pass until you report branch_clean AND the
  tree is green — so under-reporting only costs a pass, and over-reporting
  lands you right back here.

prompt campaign_user:
  Repository: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  BRANCH BASE (review the diff THIS branch introduces on top of it):
  {{vars.base_ref}}
  (On a cloud checkout a base that is not the repository's default
  branch exists only as `origin/{{vars.base_ref}}` — wherever this
  prompt says `{{vars.base_ref}}`, use whichever of the two refs
  `git rev-parse --verify` accepts; do not fetch to find out.)

  Read the diff first: `git add -N .` then
  `git diff $(git merge-base {{vars.base_ref}} HEAD)` (merge-base vs WORKING
  TREE — a prior pass's uncommitted fixes stay in scope; commit-only
  `{{vars.base_ref}}...HEAD` would hide them). Enumerate the footprint with
  `git diff $(git merge-base {{vars.base_ref}} HEAD) --name-only`. That diff
  is your scope — do NOT flag pre-existing code the branch didn't touch.

  Piloting for this run: {{vars.pilot}}

  Extra context / notes (empty = the diff speaks for itself):
  {{vars.scope_notes}}

  PRIOR REVIEW to start from (empty = none; when present, a reviewer already
  read this PR — treat each finding as a high-signal head start: verify it
  against the CURRENT diff, FIX the real ones and commit, then keep reviewing
  for anything it missed — do NOT just trust it). Each finding opens with a
  STABLE id: report on every one of them in `finding_ledger`, and honour any
  instruction about a specific id in the notes above (the author may have
  already arbitrated one, e.g. "skip R7a3f, false positive"). A finding that
  ships a `replacement` comes with the reviewer's own patch — check it still
  fits the current code, then use it rather than re-deriving one:
  {{vars.prior_review}}

  BASELINE — pre-existing failures / known-flaky tests to SKIP (empty =
  establish it once cheaply against {{vars.base_ref}}, then skip what predates
  the branch):
  {{vars.baseline}}

  Deterministic gate feedback from the PREVIOUS pass (empty on the first pass;
  when present, the build/test tree was RED after your last pass — FIX exactly
  what this shows before continuing to review the diff):
  {{input.fail_log}}

  TRIAGE PLAN (empty = no plan phase ran, plan in stride as usual; on
  continuation passes read `git log` instead). Provenance — who
  authored it and whether a cross-model peer challenged it (empty = no
  plan):
  {{input.plan_provenance}}
  The plan is a MAP, not a contract — reality wins; note where you
  diverge:
  {{input.plan}}

  Peer critique of that plan (cross-model reviewer; the plan author
  already challenged it and integrated what held — weigh what remains
  on its merits, empty = none or the review was skipped):
  {{input.plan_critique}}

  Plan author's responses to the critique (accepted/rejected and why):
  {{input.plan_responses}}

  Review the branch diff and improve it one issue at a time — locate →
  smallest fix → build → test → commit — until a fresh re-review finds no real
  issue remaining. Read `git log` first to see what earlier passes already
  fixed. Then report the termination contract (branch_clean, commits_this_pass,
  issues_remaining).

## ─── supervisor (Persy, the perseverance coach) ─────────────────────
## Concurrent LLM watcher on the campaign node enforcing the contract's
## PERSISTENCE clause from outside. Monitors are pre-seeded (armed from
## the run's first event); see docs/supervisors.md for the knobs and the
## per-run kill switch. Every text marker is pinned to `assistant_text`
## — the agent's own words — so tool output and prompt echoes never wake
## an eval (measured: 5 of 10 evals burned on echoes when unpinned).
supervisor persy:
  watches: [campaign]
  system: persy_policy
  cooldown: "5m"
  max_evals: 10
  monitors: ["event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=impossible", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=unsolvable", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=infeasib", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=not feasible", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=give up", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=giving up", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=gave up", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=workaround", "event_type=budget_warning", "event_type=budget_exceeded"]

prompt persy_policy:
  You are Persy, a perseverance coach watching a senior engineer agent
  (the `campaign` node) review and improve the changes a branch
  introduces over its base, committing each fix in stride. Your ONLY
  job is to keep it on the durable path and moving to completion — the
  push a good human operator supplies when watching the session live.
  The agent's own contract already states the policy (its PERSISTENCE
  clause); you enforce it from outside when the agent drifts.

  STAY SILENT while progress is steady — the diff being read, fixes
  landing, builds passing, commits landing. Silence is your default;
  most wakes end with intervene: false. Your monitors are pre-seeded;
  register more only when the run shows you a sharper signal.

  INTERVENE with ONE short message (2–4 sentences, specific and
  actionable, like an operator typing mid-session) only on:
  1. GIVING UP — a real issue declared unfixable, or quietly deferred
     without evidence. Demand one more INSTRUMENTED attempt (add debug
     output, shrink the repro, bisect) and name the cheapest next
     experiment visible in the recent events. Evidence decides, not
     fatigue. A finding REFUSED with evidence is not giving up.
  2. EXPEDIENT PATH — a façade, a skipped or weakened test, a
     temporary patch where the durable fix is reachable this pass.
     Name the durable alternative and ask for it.
  3. FAILURE LOOP — the same command failing repeatedly with no change
     of approach. Ask for a different approach, not another retry.
  4. PRESSURE — a budget warning or a visibly long pass. Tell it to
     BANK NOW: commit what is green, write a precise issues_remaining
     and a complete finding_ledger, leave the tree clean — before
     attempting anything else.

  NEVER: contest an honest termination report — branch_clean, or a
  precise issues_remaining with evidence, is a valid outcome
  (completion is the goal, not perpetual motion); enlarge the scope
  beyond the branch diff; re-raise a point the agent already answered
  with evidence; stack a second message on a signal you already
  steered. Once the agent reports its structured termination output,
  you are done.

## ─── verify (deterministic build/test gate, stack-agnostic) ─────────
## An ADAPTIVE agent (verify_build) reads the verify-build skill and writes
## the repo's real build+test into <scratch_dir>/verify.sh; a DETERMINISTIC
## tool node (verify_run) re-runs it and gates on the REAL exit code. No
## language / package manager is named here — the gate stays universal while
## the verdict stays deterministic. verify_build does NOT fix code: a RED gate
## routes back to `campaign` (the one capable agent) to fix what it broke.

prompt verify_system:
  You are a release engineer. A campaign pass just improved changes across
  this repository and committed them. Your single job: capture how THIS repo
  builds and tests itself into a re-runnable script the deterministic gate
  will execute. You do NOT change code.

  STEPS:
  1. Detect how THIS repo builds and tests itself — read the `verify-build`
     skill. Use the repo's OWN toolchain (a task runner / Makefile target, a
     package-manager test script, or a language build+test command),
     HONOURING any pinned toolchain (version managers, lockfiles, devbox,
     GOTOOLCHAIN). Do NOT assume a language.
  2. Write an executable POSIX-sh script to `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh`
     (an OUT-OF-TREE scratch path, NOT inside the repo — create it first with
     `mkdir -p {{vars.scratch_dir}}`) that runs the repo's build AND its tests
     (or the closest fast equivalent) and exits non-zero on any failure. Keep
     it to the commands a developer of THIS repo would run; no network-heavy
     or destructive steps.
  3. PRE-EXISTING failures: some repos are already red on the base tree,
     before this run touched anything. Scope `verify.sh` to the build plus the
     test targets the campaign's changes actually affect (package/module
     level), so the gate measures THIS run's work and a red base can't absorb
     it — and list any excluded pre-existing failures in your summary.
  4. Run it ONCE to confirm the script itself is valid (the commands resolve).
     You do NOT fix code here: if the build is RED because the campaign broke
     something, the deterministic gate reports it and the campaign fixes it on
     the next pass. Just make `verify.sh` faithfully run the repo's real
     build+test.
  5. If the repo genuinely has no build/test system (pure docs/config), write
     a script that echoes that and exits 0, and say so.

  A deterministic tool node RE-RUNS your script and gates the run on its real
  exit code. Output prepared=true once `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh` exists
  and reflects the repo's real build+test; summary = the command you settled
  on and any pre-existing failures you scoped out.

prompt verify_user:
  Repository to verify: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  Capture the repo's real build + test into `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh`
  (an out-of-tree scratch path — see the system prompt + the `verify-build`
  skill). Do NOT change code; the deterministic gate runs your script and the
  campaign fixes any red on the next pass.

  MANDATORY (skill section 1b): if the repo's own CI enforces a
  codegen-drift gate (git diff --exit-code after a regen/vendor step, a
  drift-check task, test -z on git status --porcelain), verify.sh MUST
  mirror it — run the repo's regen command(s), then fail on drift. The
  deterministic precheck rejects a gateless verify.sh in that case
  (exit 3) and the loop burns a full pass re-learning this.

## ─── PR finalization prompts (opt-in) ────────────────────────────

prompt review_system:
  You are an adversarial code reviewer running as a stage INSIDE the branch
  loop, BEFORE the work is pushed back / opened as a PR. Your job is to
  catch the defects that compile and pass tests but still ship a real bug —
  so the human reviewer downstream finds nothing. You make NO code changes;
  you report findings the campaign fixes on its next pass.

  Read the `code-review-invariants` skill FIRST. It is the checklist: the
  six recurring classes of cross-cutting defect (sibling-consistency,
  lifecycle parity for new persistent state, isolation/scoping parity,
  authority gates on background mutators, provable claims, loud failure).

  Procedure:
    1. If `build_passed` is false, the tree is mid-implementation — return
       `clean: true` with empty findings immediately (the build failure is
       handled by the deterministic gate; do not review a broken tree).
    2. Review the branch's own work: `git diff $(git merge-base <base_ref or
       the repo default branch> HEAD)` and the matching `--stat` for the
       whole footprint.
    3. For each new/changed unit, walk the invariant checklist. VERIFY a
       suspected violation against the code (open the sibling, confirm the
       boundary is really absent) before reporting — a false finding wastes
       a whole pass.
    4. `prior_findings` carries what you flagged last pass. Do NOT re-raise
       unless the current diff still shows it unaddressed.

  Blocking bar — be disciplined, this loop must converge:
    - Block (`clean: false`) ONLY on a HIGH-CONFIDENCE defect in the six
      invariant classes: a real correctness, security/isolation, lifecycle,
      authority, false-claim, or silent-failure bug you have verified.
    - Do NOT block on style, naming, preference, or "could be nicer". A diff
      with no new persistent state, no new access path, no background
      mutator, no over-claim, consistent with siblings, is CLEAN — say so.
      A false block costs a whole campaign pass; under-flagging a nit is free.

  Output: `clean` (true when nothing blocking) and `findings` (when not
  clean: specific, actionable — file:line, which invariant, why it is a real
  bug, the fix). Empty when clean.

prompt review_user:
  Review the working tree at {{vars.workspace_dir}}.
  Base: {{input.base_ref}}   ·   build passed: {{input.build_passed}}

  Findings you raised last pass (do not re-raise unless still unaddressed):
  {{input.prior_findings}}

  Adversarially review this branch's diff against the `code-review-invariants`
  checklist. Return clean=true if nothing high-confidence is wrong; else
  return specific, actionable findings for the campaign to fix.

prompt finalize_mr_system:
  You finalize a converged branch-improvement run by opening ONE pull
  request (PR; merge request on GitLab) for the SERIES of per-pass
  commits this run produced, then
  (optionally) posting the PR link back to the source issue. You make NO
  further code changes.

  Read the `forge-mr-create` skill FIRST — it tells you how to detect the
  forge, authenticate from the mounted forge_token, push the branch, and open
  the PR (GitHub via gh, GitLab via glab, Forgejo/Gitea via REST).

  Procedure:
    1. Confirm there are commits to ship: the run's HEAD must be ahead of the
       base (`git rev-list --count origin/<base>..HEAD` > 0). If not, set
       opened=false with skipped_reason "no commits ahead of base" — do NOT
       open an empty PR.
    2. Determine the branch name (mr_branch if set, else a stable
       iterion/improve/<run-id> name) and the base (mr_base if set, else
       base_ref).
    3. Authenticate the matching forge CLI from the mounted forge_token file
       when present; otherwise assume host auth. If neither is available, set
       opened=false with a precise skipped_reason — never pretend.
    4. Push HEAD to the branch on the origin remote, then open the PR from
       that branch into the base, with a clear title + body summarising the
       branch improvements landed (use scope_notes for context).
    5. If source_issue_ref is set, post the opened PR URL back to it: a forge
       issue URL → comment via the same forge CLI; a "native:<id>" ref → use
       the board comment tool. Set back_linked accordingly.

  Return opened, url, branch, back_linked, skipped_reason and a short summary.
  Be honest: report exactly what happened.

prompt finalize_mr_user:
  Workspace: {{input.workspace_dir}}
  Desired branch (may be empty → derive): {{input.mr_branch}}
  Base (may be empty → base_ref): {{input.mr_base}}
  Source issue to back-link (may be empty): {{input.source_issue_ref}}
  Context / scope: {{input.scope_notes}}

  Push the branch, open the PR, post the back-link when a source issue is
  given, and return the structured result.

## ─── Schemas ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## campaign's input: the deterministic gate's failure log from the PREVIOUS
## pass (empty on the first pass and on a green-but-more-work continuation).
schema campaign_input:
  fail_log: string
  ## Plan-phase hand-off (empty when the phase is off / skipped / on
  ## continuation passes — git is the durable state there).
  plan: string
  plan_critique: string
  plan_responses: string
  ## Who authored the plan and whether a cross-model peer challenged it:
  ## a stamp set on each hand-off edge, empty when no plan ran.
  plan_provenance: string

## campaign's TERMINATION CONTRACT (G2): a machine-checkable output the engine
## keys convergence on. branch_clean is the done-oracle (a fresh re-review of
## the branch diff finds no remaining real issue); the rest is observability.
schema campaign_output:
  branch_clean: bool
  commits_this_pass: int
  issues_remaining: string
  needs_human: bool
  human_note: string
  summary: string
  ## finding_ledger: what happened to each finding of the PRIOR REVIEW, keyed by
  ## the stable id it was shown under (on the PR comment and in prior_review).
  ## Empty when no prior review was handed over.
  ##
  ## A JSON array of {id, status, note, commit} where status is one of:
  ##   fixed    — addressed; commit carries the sha
  ##   refused  — judged not a real problem; note MUST argue why, it is read by
  ##              a human and by the next review
  ##   deferred — real but out of scope for this run; note says what it needs
  ##
  ## This is the reply half of the hand-off. Without it the review flows one
  ## way: the author is shown N findings and then told only "hardened this PR",
  ## with no way to see which of the N were answered and which were dismissed.
  ## A refusal in particular must be ARGUABLE, never silent — under mono
  ## topology nothing cross-confirms a finding, so the refusal channel is the
  ## only place a wrong finding can be contested in the open.
  finding_ledger: json

## verify_plan is verify_build's contract: it has WRITTEN <scratch_dir>/verify.sh.
schema verify_plan:
  prepared: bool
  summary: string

## verify_result is the DETERMINISTIC gate's contract. `passed` is set from
## the real process exit code of re-running <scratch_dir>/verify.sh — NOT an
## LLM judgment. `skipped` is true only when no verify script was produced
## (treated as pass but surfaced loudly, never a silent façade).
schema verify_result:
  passed: bool
  skipped: bool
  exit_code: int
  log_tail: string

## verify_probe output — deterministic "is a usable verify.sh already prepared?"
## so verify_build (an LLM agent, ~$0.69/13k tok EVERY pass) is only paid when
## the script is missing/invalid or on the first pass. fresh=true reuses it.
## verify_probe input — the continuation-loop iteration, mapped on the
## campaign→verify_probe edge. Loop refs ({{loop.*}}) do NOT resolve inside a
## tool command (only input/vars/run do), so the iteration is passed in as node
## input via the edge data-mapping. Pass 1 = iteration 0 (always regenerate).
schema verify_probe_input:
  iteration: int

schema verify_probe_state:
  fresh: bool
  reason: string

## gate is the deterministic continuation decision. converged = the tree is
## green AND the campaign reported the branch diff clean. fail_log relays the
## real build failure to the campaign on a RED pass (empty when green).
schema gate_state:
  converged: bool
  fail_log: string

## review is the in-loop adversarial self-review (ADR: push the downstream
## review INTO the loop so the pushed-back branch ships pre-refined).
schema review_input:
  base_ref: string
  build_passed: bool
  prior_findings: string

schema review_output:
  clean: bool
  findings: string

schema mr_gate_output:
  open_mr: bool
  push_back: bool

## push_back_tool output — deterministic result of the PR-branch push.
schema push_state:
  pushed: bool
  reason: string

## publish_verdict I/O — the deterministic verdict + merge-gate status.
schema post_feedback_input:
  pr_url: string
  review_summary: string
  branch_clean: bool
  commits_pushed: bool
  push_reason: string
  ## Did the DETERMINISTIC build/test gate end green, and did it actually RUN?
  ## Part of the merge-gate count: a tree that does not build is not mergeable
  ## whatever the findings, and a build that was skipped proves nothing at all.
  verify_ok: bool
  verify_skipped: bool
  ## The prior review's findings and what became of each — rendered verbatim as
  ## a table so the author sees, per finding they were shown, whether it was
  ## fixed, refused (with the argument) or deferred.
  finding_ledger: json

schema post_feedback_output:
  posted: bool
  note: string

## forge_auth_probe output — deterministic "can we push?" so finalize_mr (an
## LLM agent) is only ever entered when a push credential actually exists.
schema forge_auth_state:
  available: bool
  reason: string

schema finalize_mr_input:
  workspace_dir: string
  mr_branch: string
  mr_base: string
  source_issue_ref: string
  scope_notes: string

schema finalize_mr_output:
  opened: bool
  url: string
  branch: string
  back_linked: bool
  skipped_reason: string
  summary: string

## ─── Nodes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## ─── Plan phase (authored triage + opt-in cross-model pair review) ──
## The plan AUTHOR (claude family, read-only) triages the branch diff;
## then, ONLY when `plan_review` resolved "on" at launch, a peer from the
## OTHER family (claw + gpt) critiques the triage against the actual
## diff and the SAME author session challenges the critique and
## integrates what holds; then the campaign fixes. ONE revision turn, no
## loop — the campaign stays the arbiter of reality (asymptote intact).
## With the review off, the campaign still gets the authored triage,
## stamped unreviewed. `plan_phase: off` skips the phase altogether.
## Either way the phase passes through plan_budget_gate before campaign.

prompt plan_system:
  You are the triage planner for a branch-review campaign. You are
  READ-ONLY: read the branch diff and produce a PLAN — change nothing.

  Read the diff first: `git add -N .` then
  `git diff $(git merge-base <base_ref> HEAD)` (merge-base vs WORKING
  TREE). That diff is the scope — do NOT plan changes to pre-existing
  code the branch didn't touch. Then write the plan as a MAP, not a
  contract — the implementer may diverge where reality disagrees.

  ON A LARGE DIFF (`input.large` true in your task below): do NOT read the
  full unified diff — it is large enough that doing so would itself spend
  the plan phase's own time/cost budget (a deterministic gate fails the
  run before the campaign starts if planning overspends its share). The
  diff stat below already gives you the footprint; pull ONLY the per-file
  hunks you need to triage (`git diff $(git merge-base <base_ref> HEAD) --
  <file> | head -n 200`), file by file, prioritizing the files with the
  largest change count first. Triage from that instead of the whole diff.

  Structured output:
    - plan: the triage — the issues you found in the diff worth fixing,
      in fix order, each naming the file/hunk and the intended fix.
    - assumptions: the load-bearing assumptions the triage rests on.
    - risks: where the triage is most likely wrong, and what to check.

prompt plan_user:
  Repository: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  BRANCH BASE (triage the diff THIS branch introduces on top of it):
  {{vars.base_ref}}
  (On a cloud checkout a base that is not the repository's default
  branch exists only as `origin/{{vars.base_ref}}` — use whichever of
  the two refs `git rev-parse --verify` accepts; do not fetch.)

  Diff footprint (git diff --stat, capped):
  {{input.diff_stat}}

  Large diff: {{input.large}} — see the system prompt's large-diff
  instruction when true.

  Extra context / notes (empty = the diff speaks for itself):
  {{vars.scope_notes}}

  PRIOR REVIEW to fold into the triage (empty = none; verify each
  finding against the CURRENT diff rather than trusting it):
  {{vars.prior_review}}

prompt plan_review_system:
  You are a peer reviewer from a DIFFERENT model family, adversarially
  reviewing a branch-diff triage plan against the actual repository.
  Your value is INDEPENDENT judgment: re-read the diff with your
  read-only tools and verify the triage's claims; do not extend its own
  reasoning.

  Hunt for: a real defect in the diff the triage MISSED; a triaged
  "issue" that is not one (false positive); a fix direction that would
  be wrong or heavier than the diff warrants; an understated risk.

  ON A LARGE DIFF (`input.large` true below): do NOT re-read the full
  unified diff — use the diff stat you are given and pull only the
  per-file hunks relevant to the claims you are checking. The plan phase
  as a whole is budget-guarded; a second full-diff read here is exactly
  the cost this adaptation exists to avoid.

  Structured output:
    - concerns: the defects you can GROUND in the diff (name the
      file/hunk per claim). Empty when the triage holds — do not
      manufacture concerns to look useful.
    - suggestions: concrete improvements, most valuable first.
    - blocking: true ONLY for a defect that would make the fixed branch
      wrong (not merely improvable).

prompt plan_review_user:
  Repository: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  BRANCH BASE: {{vars.base_ref}} (on a cloud checkout a non-default
  base exists only as `origin/{{vars.base_ref}}` — use whichever of the
  two refs `git rev-parse --verify` accepts; do not fetch)
  Read the diff yourself: `git add -N .` then
  `git diff $(git merge-base {{vars.base_ref}} HEAD)` — or, on a large
  diff (see below), the stat + targeted per-file hunks instead.

  Diff footprint (git diff --stat, capped):
  {{input.diff_stat}}

  Large diff: {{input.large}}

  THE TRIAGE PLAN under review:
  {{input.plan}}

  Its stated assumptions:
  {{input.assumptions}}

  Its stated risks:
  {{input.risks}}

prompt plan_revise_system:
  You authored the triage plan earlier in this session. A peer reviewer
  from another model family has critiqued it. CHALLENGE the critique:
  verify each concern against the diff, integrate what holds, refuse
  what does not — with a stated reason. You revise the PLAN only; you
  change no code.

  Structured output:
    - plan: the final triage plan, revised where the critique held.
    - review_responses: one line per concern — accepted (and how the
      plan changed) or rejected (and why).

prompt plan_revise_user:
  Peer critique of your plan:
  {{input.concerns}}

  Peer suggestions:
  {{input.suggestions}}

  Blocking: {{input.blocking}}

  Your plan as reviewed:
  {{input.plan}}

## workspace_probe output — the deterministic "is there a repository to
## work on, with the base reachable?" verdict. ok=false carries the typed
## code WORKSPACE_NOT_A_REPO and a reason naming what was refused.
schema workspace_probe_state:
  ok: bool
  code: string
  reason: string

schema plan_topology_state:
  do_plan: bool

schema plan_review_topology_state:
  do_review: bool

## plan_scope_state is plan_scope_probe's contract: the diff footprint +
## size classification, which drives the large-diff adaptation in
## plan_system/plan_review_system.
schema plan_scope_state:
  diff_stat: string
  large: bool

schema plan_input:
  diff_stat: string
  large: bool

schema plan_output:
  plan: string
  assumptions: string
  risks: string

schema plan_review_input:
  plan: string
  assumptions: string
  risks: string
  diff_stat: string
  large: bool

schema plan_review_output:
  concerns: string
  suggestions: string
  blocking: bool

## skipped: the peer literally did not serve (skip route fired -- credential
## dead, policy=skip). skip_revise: skip the author-revise turn regardless
## of why -- either the peer never served (nothing to revise against) OR
## the diff is large enough that a second full read would itself blow the
## plan phase's own budget share. The two stay distinct because they are
## different operational stories even though both bypass plan_revise.
schema plan_gate_state:
  skipped: bool
  skip_revise: bool
  ## The provenance stamp for the skip_revise route, naming WHICH of the
  ## two stories applies (peer skipped vs. revise bypassed on a large
  ## diff) — the campaign reads it, so the two must not blur.
  provenance: string

schema plan_revise_input:
  plan: string
  assumptions: string
  risks: string
  concerns: string
  suggestions: string
  blocking: bool

schema plan_revised_output:
  plan: string
  review_responses: string

## plan_budget_gate's contract. It is the single choke point before
## campaign, so it receives the plan/critique/responses text from
## whichever of the three upstream routes produced it (review off,
## skip_revise, plan_revise) and relays it onward -- the campaign reads
## the same fields whether or not the guard let it through.
schema plan_budget_gate_input:
  plan: string
  plan_critique: string
  plan_responses: string
  plan_provenance: string

## exhausted is the deterministic verdict; the four figures beside it are
## the comparison it rests on, in the units the plan_exhausted fail node
## reports to the operator. A share is `cap x plan_budget_ratio`, and a
## cap of 0 means UNBOUNDED on that axis -- so its share is 0 and the
## matching over_* stays false.
schema plan_budget_gate_state:
  exhausted: bool
  over_duration: bool
  over_cost: bool
  elapsed_seconds: float
  duration_share_seconds: float
  spent_usd: float
  cost_share_usd: float
  plan: string
  plan_critique: string
  plan_responses: string
  plan_provenance: string

## workspace_probe — DETERMINISTIC precondition and the run's ENTRY: a
## launch with nothing to work on is refused BEFORE any LLM node spends
## (a `--bot` launch carrying only pr_url attaches no repository; the
## plan node would otherwise triage a diff against nothing). Refuses
## with the typed code WORKSPACE_NOT_A_REPO in two shapes: workspace_dir
## is absent or is not a git repository (`git rev-parse --git-dir`
## fails); or the repository exists but base_ref resolves nowhere, or
## shares no history with HEAD — every diff-anchored instruction of the
## campaign would then range over nothing. base_ref is RESOLVED, never
## fetched: the bare name first, then refs/remotes/origin/<base_ref> —
## a cloud PR run's checkout (`git clone --no-tags` + a fetch of the PR
## head) carries only the default branch and the head as local branches,
## so a PR targeting any other branch has its base ONLY as a
## remote-tracking ref. The verdict is the node's OUTPUT (ok/code/
## reason, routed to the named `workspace_not_a_repo` fail node, which stamps that code on the RUN) and is echoed on stderr for
## the tool log; the process exits 0 on purpose — a non-zero exit would
## replace the typed verdict with the engine's generic tool failure.
## python3 for safe JSON (mirrors verify_run); read-only, ~100ms.
tool workspace_probe:
  description: "Precondition: workspace_dir is a git repository with base_ref resolvable and reachable"
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} BASE={{vars.base_ref}} python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json, sys
ws = (os.environ.get('WS') or '').strip()
base = (os.environ.get('BASE') or '').strip()
def refuse(reason):
    sys.stderr.write('WORKSPACE_NOT_A_REPO: ' + reason + chr(10))
    print(json.dumps({'ok': False, 'code': 'WORKSPACE_NOT_A_REPO', 'reason': reason}))
    raise SystemExit(0)
def git(*a):
    return subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws] + list(a), stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True, timeout=60)
def resolve_base(name):
    tried = [name, 'refs/remotes/origin/' + name]
    for ref in tried:
        if git('rev-parse', '--verify', '-q', ref + '^{commit}').returncode == 0:
            return ref, tried
    return '', tried
if not ws or not os.path.isdir(ws):
    refuse('workspace_dir ' + repr(ws) + ' does not exist')
p = git('rev-parse', '--git-dir')
if p.returncode != 0:
    refuse('workspace_dir ' + repr(ws) + ' is not a git repository (git rev-parse --git-dir: ' + (p.stderr or '').strip()[-300:] + ')')
resolved = ''
if base:
    resolved, tried = resolve_base(base)
    if not resolved:
        refuse('base_ref ' + repr(base) + ' does not resolve in ' + ws + ' (tried ' + ', '.join(tried) + '; a cloud checkout carries a non-default base only as refs/remotes/origin/<base>, and the probe never fetches)')
    m = git('merge-base', resolved, 'HEAD')
    if m.returncode != 0:
        refuse('base_ref ' + repr(base) + ' (resolved as ' + resolved + ') shares no history with HEAD in ' + ws + ' (git merge-base: ' + (m.stderr or '').strip()[-300:] + ')')
print(json.dumps({'ok': True, 'code': '', 'reason': 'git repository at ' + ws + ', base ' + (repr(base) + ' resolved as ' + resolved + ' and' if base else '(none)') + ' reachable'}))
"`
  output: workspace_probe_state

## plan_topology lifts the plan_phase switch into a bool so the entry
## edges use the C012-exhaustive simple form. No LLM. This gate is the
## PHASE (on by default); whether a cross-model peer reviews the authored
## triage is decided after it exists, by plan_review_topology.
compute plan_topology:
  description: "Plan phase gate (plan_phase switch)"
  output: plan_topology_state
  expr:
    do_plan: "vars.plan_phase == 'on'"

## plan_scope_probe — DETERMINISTIC pre-flight for the plan phase: the diff
## footprint (git diff --stat, capped) + a large-diff classification, which
## drives the adaptation in plan_system/plan_review_system. Pure git
## plumbing -- no language / package-manager assumption, stack- and
## repo-agnostic. python3 for safe JSON (mirrors verify_probe/verify_run);
## read-only, idempotent.
tool plan_scope_probe:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} BASE={{vars.base_ref}} THRESH={{vars.plan_large_diff_lines}} python3 -c "
import os, re, subprocess, json
ws = os.environ.get('WS', '.')
base = os.environ.get('BASE', 'main')
try:
    threshold = int(os.environ.get('THRESH', '1500') or '1500')
except ValueError:
    threshold = 1500
def run(*a):
    return subprocess.run(list(a), cwd=ws, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
# The base resolves exactly as in workspace_probe, which has already
# refused a base that resolves nowhere: the bare name first, then the
# remote-tracking ref a cloud clone carries for a non-default branch.
# Never a fetch.
ref = base
for cand in (base, 'refs/remotes/origin/' + base):
    if run('git', 'rev-parse', '--verify', '-q', cand + '^{commit}').returncode == 0:
        ref = cand
        break
mb = run('git', 'merge-base', ref, 'HEAD')
base_sha = (mb.stdout or '').strip() or ref
stat = run('git', 'diff', '--stat', base_sha)
shortstat = run('git', 'diff', '--shortstat', base_sha)
added = 0
m = re.search(r'(\d+) insertion', shortstat.stdout or '')
if m:
    added = int(m.group(1))
diff_stat = (stat.stdout or '')[:8000]
print(json.dumps({
    'diff_stat': diff_stat,
    'large': added >= threshold,
}))
"`
  output: plan_scope_state

## plan — the AUTHOR. Same family/model as the campaign (the peer is the
## cross-check). readonly is enforced on codex/pi only — on claude_code
## (bypassPermissions) it is intent, not a guarantee; the deterministic
## verify gate + the campaign's own review remain the backstop.
agent plan:
  description: "Author the triage plan (read-only)"
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  input: plan_input
  output: plan_output
  system: plan_system
  user: plan_user
  session: fresh
  readonly: true

## plan_review_topology — is a cross-model peer wired for this run? Lifts
## the launch-resolved plan_review var (auto → on iff a second model
## family is credentialed) into a bool AFTER the triage is authored, so
## an edge condition can read it. off → the authored triage goes on to
## the budget gate and the campaign unreviewed, stamped so the campaign
## knows no peer looked at it. No LLM.
compute plan_review_topology:
  description: "Peer review gate (launch-resolved plan_review)"
  output: plan_review_topology_state
  expr:
    do_review: "vars.plan_review == 'on'"

## plan_review — the PEER, pinned to the other family. claw because
## "openai/*" models only resolve there; both knobs env-overridable so a
## host without OpenAI quota can run the peer elsewhere. Read-only
## toolset (no write_file/file_edit tools — though bash can write; the
## restriction is intent + review-pr's shipped posture, not a sandbox).
judge plan_review:
  description: "Peer plan review (other model family)"
  backend: "${ITERION_PLAN_REVIEW_BACKEND_GPT:-claw}"
  model: "${ITERION_PLAN_REVIEW_MODEL_GPT:-openai/gpt-5.6-sol}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_GPT:-high}"
  input: plan_review_input
  output: plan_review_output
  system: plan_review_system
  user: plan_review_user
  session: fresh
  tools: [bash, read_file, glob, grep]
  tool_max_steps: 30
  fallbacks:
    ## The plan_review_policy switch, expressed as a route gate:
    ## policy "skip" arms this terminal route — a peer failure completes
    ## the node with a zero-value critique stamped _skipped (plan_gate
    ## routes around the revise turn). policy "wait" leaves NO active
    ## route: the failure stays failed_resumable and the run-level
    ## usage-window retry parks the run until the forfait reopens.
    ## on: [any] — deliberately unfiltered: the operator's `skip` means
    ## the peer must NEVER block the campaign, whatever killed it. It is
    ## also load-bearing under the shipped `sandbox: auto`: a sandboxed
    ## claw failure flattens to a string at the IPC boundary and
    ## classifies UNCLASSIFIED, which a filtered skip refuses by design.
    give_up:
      action: skip
      when: "vars.plan_review_policy == 'skip'"
      on: [any]

## plan_gate — did the peer actually serve, or did the skip route fire? And
## should the author-revise turn run at all? Deterministic: reads the
## _skipped stamp + plan_scope_probe's large flag, never an LLM judgment.
## skip_revise bypasses plan_revise on EITHER signal: nothing to revise
## against (peer skipped) or revising would itself blow the plan phase's
## budget share (large diff, native:695).
compute plan_gate:
  description: "Peer review served or skipped? Large-diff revise bypass?"
  output: plan_gate_state
  expr:
    skipped: "outputs.plan_review._skipped == true"
    skip_revise: "outputs.plan_review._skipped == true || outputs.plan_scope_probe.large"
    provenance: "if(outputs.plan_review._skipped == true, 'authored by the plan node - the cross-model peer review was SKIPPED mid-run (peer unavailable, plan_review_policy skip); the author-written triage, unchallenged', 'authored by the plan node and critiqued by a cross-model peer; the author-revise turn was BYPASSED on a large diff, so the critique reaches you unrevised - weigh it on its merits')"

## plan_revise — the AUTHOR again, SAME session (the `_session_id`
## carried on the edge), challenging the peer's critique. Read-only.
agent plan_revise:
  description: "Author challenges & integrates the critique"
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  input: plan_revise_input
  output: plan_revised_output
  system: plan_revise_system
  user: plan_revise_user
  session: inherit_if_available
  readonly: true

## plan_budget_gate — the SOLE choke point before campaign (native:695).
## ONE deterministic compute, no shell and no clock of its own: the run's
## `run` namespace carries its consumption (elapsed_seconds, cost_usd) AND
## the caps IN FORCE (max_duration_seconds, max_cost_usd -- after any
## --max-duration/--max-cost-usd override, the recipe, the platform
## ceiling and any live raise_budget), so the comparison cannot drift from
## the budget: block the way a mirror var did. exhausted=true routes to
## the typed `plan_exhausted` fail instead of letting campaign start on
## whatever budget the plan phase left behind -- GUARANTEEING campaign at
## least (1-ratio) of the run's budget whenever it does start.
##
## The gate is also the relay: it receives plan/critique/responses from
## whichever of the three upstream routes produced them and hands the same
## fields to campaign. Nothing but the plan phase has spent when it runs,
## so run.cost_usd IS the phase's spend -- including anything a hand-written
## per-node sum would have forgotten.
compute plan_budget_gate:
  description: "Plan-phase budget guard (the sole choke point before campaign)"
  input: plan_budget_gate_input
  output: plan_budget_gate_state
  expr:
    ## A cap of 0 is UNBOUNDED on that axis, never "no allowance left":
    ## each comparison is guarded on a positive cap, so an unbudgeted run
    ## (or one capped on the other axis only) never refuses here.
    over_duration: "run.max_duration_seconds > 0 && run.elapsed_seconds > run.max_duration_seconds * vars.plan_budget_ratio"
    over_cost: "run.max_cost_usd > 0 && run.cost_usd > run.max_cost_usd * vars.plan_budget_ratio"
    ## Repeated rather than referenced: a compute's fields are evaluated
    ## against the node's input and the run, never against its own
    ## siblings, so `over_duration || over_cost` would read nil.
    exhausted: "(run.max_duration_seconds > 0 && run.elapsed_seconds > run.max_duration_seconds * vars.plan_budget_ratio) || (run.max_cost_usd > 0 && run.cost_usd > run.max_cost_usd * vars.plan_budget_ratio)"
    elapsed_seconds: "run.elapsed_seconds"
    duration_share_seconds: "run.max_duration_seconds * vars.plan_budget_ratio"
    spent_usd: "run.cost_usd"
    cost_share_usd: "run.max_cost_usd * vars.plan_budget_ratio"
    plan: "input.plan"
    plan_critique: "input.plan_critique"
    plan_responses: "input.plan_responses"
    plan_provenance: "input.plan_provenance"

## plan_exhausted — the plan phase outgrew its share. resumable: true
## because the cure is "widen the budget and carry on": a terminal failure
## would make the operator re-pay a plan phase this run already completed,
## which is the very cost the guard exists to avoid. The checkpoint
## anchors on plan_budget_gate, so the resume re-evaluates the guard
## against the caps then in force and takes the campaign edge.
fail plan_exhausted:
  description: "the plan phase outgrew its share of the run's budget"
  code: PLAN_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED
  message: "the plan phase used {{outputs.plan_budget_gate.elapsed_seconds}}s of its {{outputs.plan_budget_gate.duration_share_seconds}}s share and USD {{outputs.plan_budget_gate.spent_usd}} of its USD {{outputs.plan_budget_gate.cost_share_usd}} share (plan_budget_ratio {{vars.plan_budget_ratio}} of the caps in force); campaign never started, so nothing is committed. Raise the caps and resume (iterion resume --run-id <id> --file <bot> --max-duration 5h --max-cost-usd 150), or lower plan_budget_ratio — the guard is re-evaluated, not replayed."
  resumable: true

## workspace_not_a_repo — the entry precondition refused. NOT resumable:
## nothing about this run changes by resuming it, because the workspace it
## was launched against is not the thing the operator can widen. Fix the
## launch (attach a repository, name a reachable base_ref) and start again.
fail workspace_not_a_repo:
  description: "the launch has no repository to work on"
  code: WORKSPACE_NOT_A_REPO
  message: "{{outputs.workspace_probe.reason}}"
  resumable: false

## campaign — the one adaptive agent. claude_code with the full native
## toolset (read/edit/shell/git) so it works exactly like a native Claude
## Code session: read the branch diff, a living todo list of real issues, one
## verified commit per fix, in stride. session: fresh — a fresh pass
## re-derives its todo list from the branch diff + `git log` (git is the
## durable state). reasoning_effort high, not max: the productive-session data
## says the working SHAPE, not the model tier, is the lever (framing >
## capability). interaction: human — the agent may pause for the operator on a
## genuine mid-flight decision (kept rare by the contract).
agent campaign:
  ## The finding ledger is handed to a later review (manifest
  ## `produces: kind: review_ledger`), and only a node that declares
  ## `publish:` leaves an artifact behind for it to read.
  publish: campaign_result
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  input: campaign_input
  output: campaign_output
  system: campaign_system
  user: campaign_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: human

## verify_probe — DETERMINISTIC pre-check: is a usable verify.sh already in the
## scratch dir from an earlier pass of THIS run? verify_build is an LLM agent
## (~$0.69/13k tok EVERY pass); once pass 1 has authored verify.sh, re-authoring
## it every pass is pure waste. This tool answers "reuse it?" in ~50ms of
## subprocess: fresh=true when verify.sh exists, is non-empty, and passes
## `sh -n` — AND this is not the first pass (iteration>0), so pass 1 of every run
## always (re)generates verify.sh for the CURRENT tree, defeating a stale script
## a prior run may have left in a persistent (non-sandbox) scratch dir. python3
## for safe JSON + no quote clashes (mirrors verify_run); read-only, idempotent.
tool verify_probe:
  command: `SCRATCH={{vars.scratch_dir}} ITER={{input.iteration}} python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json
scratch = os.environ.get('SCRATCH', '.')
script = os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.sh')
try:
    iteration = int(os.environ.get('ITER', '0') or '0')
except ValueError:
    iteration = 0
fresh = False
if iteration <= 0:
    reason = 'first pass of this run: (re)generate verify.sh for the current tree'
elif not os.path.isfile(script):
    reason = 'no verify.sh in the scratch dir yet'
elif os.path.getsize(script) == 0:
    reason = 'verify.sh is empty'
else:
    chk = subprocess.run(['sh', '-n', script], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True)
    if chk.returncode != 0:
        reason = 'verify.sh failed the sh -n syntax check: ' + (chk.stdout or '')[-300:]
    else:
        fresh = True
        reason = 'verify.sh present and valid; reuse it (skip verify_build)'
print(json.dumps({'fresh': fresh, 'reason': reason}))
"`
  input: verify_probe_input
  output: verify_probe_state

## verify_build — adaptive, stack-agnostic gate authoring (writes
## <scratch_dir>/verify.sh). fresh: whole-tree buildability, not a per-fix
## session. high, not max: mechanical capture of the repo's build/test.
agent verify_build:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_VERIFY_BUILD:-medium}"
  output: verify_plan
  system: verify_system
  user: verify_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: human

## verify_run — the DETERMINISTIC half: re-runs the agent-prepared
## <scratch_dir>/verify.sh and reports the REAL exit code. No language /
## package manager appears here — it executes whatever verify_build wrote, so
## the gate stays universal while the verdict stays deterministic. python3 for
## safe JSON + subprocess capture; double-quote-free body, no backticks (E012).
tool verify_run:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} SCRATCH={{vars.scratch_dir}} python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json
ws = os.environ.get('WS', '.')
scratch = os.environ.get('SCRATCH', '.')
try:
    os.makedirs(scratch, exist_ok=True)
except OSError:
    pass
script = os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.sh')
if not os.path.exists(script):
    print(json.dumps({'passed': True, 'skipped': True, 'exit_code': 0, 'log_tail': 'verify_build produced no verify.sh in the scratch dir; build/test gate SKIPPED (counted as pass, but surfaced) -- verify_build should write the script'}))
    raise SystemExit(0)
def tree_state():
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws, 'status', '--porcelain'], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, timeout=60)
        if p.returncode != 0:
            return None
        return set(l for l in p.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip())
    except Exception:
        return None
pre = tree_state()
rc = 0
out = ''
try:
    p = subprocess.run(['sh', script], cwd=ws, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, timeout=1500)
    out = p.stdout or ''
    rc = p.returncode
except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as e:
    out = (e.output or '') + chr(10) + '[verify timed out after 1500s]'
    rc = 124
except Exception as e:
    out = 'verify_run could not execute the script: ' + str(e)
    rc = 1
# Deterministic codegen-drift tail (stack-agnostic; the gate stays
# deterministic per ADR-044). Only on a GREEN verify: (1) if the repo's own
# CI enforces a drift gate, verify.sh must mirror it; (2) a green verify
# must not leave new changes in the tree (regen output belongs in a commit).
if rc == 0:
    import glob
    def has_drift_gate(text):
        for l in text.splitlines():
            # git diff --exit-code is the canonical build-failing gate.
            if 'diff' in l and '--exit-code' in l:
                return True
            # --quiet / status --porcelain also appear as commit-if-changed
            # control flow in updater workflows (e.g. a Homebrew tap bump) --
            # only count them when the same line actually fails the build.
            gated = ('exit 1' in l) or ('|| exit' in l) or ('test -z' in l) or ('[ -z' in l)
            if gated and (('diff' in l and '--quiet' in l) or 'status --porcelain' in l):
                return True
        return False
    ci_hit = ''
    for pat in ('.github/workflows/*.yml', '.github/workflows/*.yaml', '.gitlab-ci.yml', '.circleci/config.yml'):
        for f in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(ws, pat))):
            try:
                if has_drift_gate(open(f, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace').read()):
                    ci_hit = os.path.relpath(f, ws)
                    break
            except OSError:
                pass
        if ci_hit:
            break
    if ci_hit:
        try:
            vs = open(script, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace').read()
        except OSError:
            vs = ''
        if not has_drift_gate(vs):
            rc = 3
            out = out + chr(10) + 'DRIFT GATE MISSING: CI (' + ci_hit + ') enforces a codegen-drift gate but ' + script + ' has none -- edit the script per the verify-build skill section 1b (run the repo regen command, then: git diff --exit-code).'
    if rc == 0 and pre is not None:
        post = tree_state()
        if post is not None:
            fresh = sorted(post - pre)
            if fresh:
                rc = 4
                out = out + chr(10) + 'UNCOMMITTED REGEN OUTPUT: a green verify left new changes in the tree; commit (or gitignore) them:' + chr(10) + chr(10).join(fresh[:50])
try:
    with open(os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.log'), 'w', encoding='utf-8') as lf:
        lf.write(out)
except OSError:
    pass
print(json.dumps({'passed': rc == 0, 'skipped': False, 'exit_code': rc, 'log_tail': out[-4000:]}))
"`
  output: verify_result

## gate — the deterministic continuation decision. Reads the real gate result
## (outputs.verify_run.passed) AND the campaign's termination output
## (outputs.campaign.branch_clean), both fresh in the loop BODY (the same
## discipline whole_improve_loop's gate uses to read the loop-head safely).
## converged = green AND the branch diff is clean. On a RED pass fail_log
## carries the real build failure to the campaign; empty when green. No LLM,
## no shell.
## review — adversarial in-loop self-review (readonly). Runs after the
## deterministic build gate; reads the code-review-invariants skill and the
## branch diff, and blocks convergence ONLY on a high-confidence defect in
## the six invariant classes. The downstream reviewer's job, moved into the
## loop so the pushed-back branch ships clean. claude_code (read/git), readonly.
agent review:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_REVIEW:-claude-opus-5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_REVIEW:-high}"
  input: review_input
  output: review_output
  system: review_system
  user: review_user
  session: fresh
  readonly: true

compute gate:
  output: gate_state
  expr:
    converged: "outputs.verify_run.passed && outputs.campaign.branch_clean && outputs.review.clean"
    fail_log: "if(outputs.verify_run.passed, if(outputs.review.clean, '', outputs.review.findings), outputs.verify_run.log_tail)"

## mr_gate routes the post-convergence tail into at most ONE of two
## mutually-exclusive booleans (the unconditional edge to done is the C012
## fallback when neither matches): open_mr (finalize path), push_back
## (PR-context: no PR wanted but a target forge branch is declared — whether
## anything NEW exists to push is decided by push_back_tool's own rev-list,
## the in-repo truth across all passes). No LLM, no shell.
compute mr_gate:
  output: mr_gate_output
  expr:
    open_mr: "vars.open_mr"
    push_back: "!vars.open_mr && vars.push_branch != ''"

## forge_auth_probe — DETERMINISTIC pre-flight for the push. finalize_mr is an
## LLM agent (claude_code, opus); entering it with NO push credential mounted
## burns a whole turn just to REDISCOVER, in shell, that it can't push (~$0.26
## observed on run 019f415c). This tool answers "is there a push credential?"
## in ~100ms of subprocess so the graph only pays finalize_mr when a push can
## actually happen. python3 for safe JSON + no quote clashes (mirrors
## verify_run); read-only, idempotent probes.
tool forge_auth_probe:
  command: `python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json
tok = '/run/iterion/secrets/forge_token'
try:
    if os.path.isfile(tok) and os.path.getsize(tok) > 0:
        print(json.dumps({'available': True, 'reason': 'file:' + tok})); raise SystemExit(0)
except OSError:
    pass
for v in ('GH_TOKEN', 'GITHUB_TOKEN', 'GITLAB_TOKEN', 'FORGEJO_TOKEN', 'GITEA_TOKEN'):
    if os.environ.get(v):
        print(json.dumps({'available': True, 'reason': 'env:' + v})); raise SystemExit(0)
try:
    r = subprocess.run(['gh', 'auth', 'status'], stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=10)
    if r.returncode == 0:
        print(json.dumps({'available': True, 'reason': 'gh host auth'})); raise SystemExit(0)
except Exception:
    pass
print(json.dumps({'available': False, 'reason': 'no forge_token secret, no *_TOKEN env, no gh host auth'}))
"`
  output: forge_auth_state

## push_auth_probe — the push-back path's copy of the credential pre-flight
## (a graph node is a unique instance, so the open_mr and push_back paths
## each carry their own probe). Same contract as forge_auth_probe.
tool push_auth_probe:
  command: `python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json
tok = '/run/iterion/secrets/forge_token'
try:
    if os.path.isfile(tok) and os.path.getsize(tok) > 0:
        print(json.dumps({'available': True, 'reason': 'file:' + tok})); raise SystemExit(0)
except OSError:
    pass
for v in ('GH_TOKEN', 'GITHUB_TOKEN', 'GITLAB_TOKEN', 'FORGEJO_TOKEN', 'GITEA_TOKEN'):
    if os.environ.get(v):
        print(json.dumps({'available': True, 'reason': 'env:' + v})); raise SystemExit(0)
try:
    r = subprocess.run(['gh', 'auth', 'status'], stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=10)
    if r.returncode == 0:
        print(json.dumps({'available': True, 'reason': 'gh host auth'})); raise SystemExit(0)
except Exception:
    pass
print(json.dumps({'available': False, 'reason': 'no forge_token secret, no *_TOKEN env, no gh host auth'}))
"`
  output: forge_auth_state

## push_back_tool — DETERMINISTIC push of the run's HEAD onto the declared
## forge branch (the PR's source branch). The in-repo truth decides: when
## rev-list shows nothing new over origin/<push_branch> it no-ops cleanly, so
## a converged-with-no-commits pass never errors. The token comes from the
## mounted forge_token secret or a *_TOKEN env and is redacted from any
## failure output; it is tried under the common forge basic-auth usernames
## (x-access-token = GitHub, oauth2 = GitLab-style). No LLM — the push is a
## mechanical act once the campaign banked the commits.
tool push_back_tool:
  command: `PUSH_BRANCH={{vars.push_branch}} python3 -c "
import os, subprocess, json, re
branch = os.environ.get('PUSH_BRANCH', '').strip()
if not branch:
    print(json.dumps({'pushed': False, 'reason': 'no push_branch declared'})); raise SystemExit(0)
def run(*a, **kw):
    return subprocess.run(list(a), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=kw.get('timeout', 120))
tok = ''
p = '/run/iterion/secrets/forge_token'
try:
    if os.path.isfile(p) and os.path.getsize(p) > 0:
        tok = open(p).read().strip()
except OSError:
    pass
if not tok:
    for v in ('GH_TOKEN', 'GITHUB_TOKEN', 'GITLAB_TOKEN', 'FORGEJO_TOKEN', 'GITEA_TOKEN'):
        if os.environ.get(v):
            tok = os.environ[v].strip(); break
if not tok:
    print(json.dumps({'pushed': False, 'reason': 'no forge token available'})); raise SystemExit(0)
r = run('git', 'remote', 'get-url', 'origin')
m = re.match(r'https://(?:[^@/]+@)?([^/]+)/(.+?)(?:\\.git)?$', (r.stdout or '').strip())
if not m:
    print(json.dumps({'pushed': False, 'reason': 'origin is not an https remote'})); raise SystemExit(0)
host, path = m.group(1), m.group(2)
urls = [(u, 'https://' + u + ':' + tok + '@' + host + '/' + path + '.git') for u in ('x-access-token', 'oauth2')]
def redact(s):
    return (s or '').strip().replace(tok, '***')[-200:]
last, rebased = '', False
# Bounded fetch -> (rebase if remote advanced) -> push loop. A non-fast-forward
# rejection is the EXPECTED case when the PR author (or another bot) pushed to
# the branch while this review ran: re-fetch, replay OUR commits onto the fresh
# remote head, and retry. Only a genuine rebase CONFLICT (the advance touched
# the same lines we fixed) falls back to a flag — that needs a human.
for attempt in range(3):
    r = run('git', 'fetch', 'origin', branch)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        print(json.dumps({'pushed': False, 'reason': 'fetch origin/' + branch + ' failed (branch may be merged or deleted -> the fixes need a NEW PR onto the base): ' + redact(r.stderr or r.stdout)})); raise SystemExit(0)
    ahead = run('git', 'rev-list', '--count', 'FETCH_HEAD..HEAD')
    n = int((ahead.stdout or '0').strip() or '0') if ahead.returncode == 0 else -1
    if n == 0:
        print(json.dumps({'pushed': False, 'reason': 'nothing to push: HEAD not ahead of origin/' + branch})); raise SystemExit(0)
    behind = run('git', 'rev-list', '--count', 'HEAD..FETCH_HEAD')
    b = int((behind.stdout or '0').strip() or '0') if behind.returncode == 0 else 0
    if b > 0:
        mb = run('git', 'merge-base', 'FETCH_HEAD', 'HEAD')
        base = (mb.stdout or '').strip()
        rb = run('git', '-c', 'rebase.autoStash=true', 'rebase', '--onto', 'FETCH_HEAD', base, 'HEAD')
        if rb.returncode != 0:
            run('git', 'rebase', '--abort')
            print(json.dumps({'pushed': False, 'reason': 'remote branch advanced by ' + str(b) + ' commit(s) that CONFLICT with these fixes (auto-rebase failed) -> needs a manual reconcile: ' + redact(rb.stderr or rb.stdout)})); raise SystemExit(0)
        rebased = True
        n = int((run('git', 'rev-list', '--count', 'FETCH_HEAD..HEAD').stdout or '0').strip() or '0')
    nonff = False
    for user, url in urls:
        r = run('git', 'push', url, 'HEAD:refs/heads/' + branch, timeout=180)
        if r.returncode == 0:
            print(json.dumps({'pushed': True, 'reason': 'pushed ' + str(n) + ' commit(s) HEAD->' + branch + ' as ' + user + (' (rebased onto advanced remote)' if rebased else '')})); raise SystemExit(0)
        last = redact(r.stderr or r.stdout)
        if 'non-fast-forward' in last or 'fetch first' in last or 'rejected' in last or 'stale info' in last:
            nonff = True; break
    if not nonff:
        print(json.dumps({'pushed': False, 'reason': 'push failed: ' + last})); raise SystemExit(0)
print(json.dumps({'pushed': False, 'reason': 'push failed after 3 rebase-retries (remote kept advancing): ' + last}))
"`
  output: push_state

## finalize_mr — opt-in PR creation shipping the SERIES of per-pass commits.
## claude_code so the bundled forge-mr-create skill is mirrored and the board
## comment tool (native back-link) is available. Only reached when
## forge_auth_probe confirmed a push credential exists.
agent finalize_mr:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_BRANCH_IMPROVE_MR_MODEL:-claude-opus-5}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_BRANCH_IMPROVE_MR_EFFORT:-medium}"
  input: finalize_mr_input
  output: finalize_mr_output
  system: finalize_mr_system
  user: finalize_mr_user
  tools: [bash, read_file, glob, grep]
  capabilities: [board.read, board.comment]
  ## 20 is ample for push + open-PR + back-link (~5-8 commands nominal); 40 was
  ## headroom for a degenerate credential-probe spin that forge_auth_probe now
  ## makes impossible (the node is only entered when a push credential exists).
  tool_max_steps: 20

## surface_pr_link — DETERMINISTIC result-link emitter for the studio run
## summary. finalize_mr is an LLM agent, but the studio surfaces a run's
## headline PR link from the deterministic `[iterion] preview_url=… kind=pr`
## directive a tool node prints on stdout — never from agent prose. Reached
## only when finalize_mr actually opened a PR (opened=true ⇒ url set); the
## python guard drops an empty url so the scanner never sees a malformed
## directive. read-only, idempotent.
schema surface_pr_link_input:
  url: string

tool surface_pr_link:
  input: surface_pr_link_input
  command: `URL={{input.url}} python3 -c "
import os
u = (os.environ.get('URL') or '').strip()
if u:
    print('[iterion] preview_url=' + u + ' kind=pr scope=external')
"`

## publish_verdict — DETERMINISTIC verdict on the PR: the finding ledger as a
## table, plus the merge-gate commit status. No LLM: the table is rendered from
## the ledger verbatim and the gate is a COUNT, so neither the wording of a
## refusal nor the merge signal depends on a model turn (same move Vetty made).
##
## Posted through the iterion server's forge-publish endpoint with the per-run
## grant it injects, so the run never holds a posting credential.
##
## THE GATE IS NEVER GREEN ON A HEAD WITH UNRESOLVED FINDINGS. A refusal is an
## argument for a human, not a way past the check: if it were, a fixer could
## clear any review by contesting every finding. Success requires all three —
## every finding fixed, a fresh re-review clean, and the deterministic build
## gate green — and says in its description that the re-review was the fixer own,
## so nobody reads it as an independent one.
##
## Keep this python body free of the double-quote character: the whole body is
## wrapped by the shell in a double-quoted argument.
tool publish_verdict:
  command: `PUB_URL={{vars.forge_publish_url}} PUB_TOKEN={{vars.forge_publish_token}} PR_URL={{input.pr_url}} LEDGER={{input.finding_ledger}} SUMMARY={{input.review_summary}} CLEAN={{input.branch_clean}} PUSHED={{input.commits_pushed}} PUSH_REASON={{input.push_reason}} VERIFY_OK={{input.verify_ok}} VERIFY_SKIPPED={{input.verify_skipped}} GATE_ENABLED={{vars.gate_enabled}} GATE_CONTEXT={{vars.gate_context}} python3 -c "
import os, json, urllib.request, urllib.error

def emit(posted, note):
    print(json.dumps({'posted': posted, 'note': note}))
    raise SystemExit(0)

def flag(name):
    return str(os.environ.get(name, '')).strip().lower() in ('1', 'true', 'yes', 'on')

nl = chr(10)
pr = os.environ.get('PR_URL', '').strip()
url = os.environ.get('PUB_URL', '').strip()
tok = os.environ.get('PUB_TOKEN', '').strip()
if not pr:
    emit(False, 'no pr_url')
if not url or not tok:
    emit(False, 'no forge publish grant on this run: launch through an iterion server whose team connection covers this repo')

try:
    ledger = json.loads(os.environ.get('LEDGER') or '[]')
except ValueError:
    ledger = []
if not isinstance(ledger, list):
    ledger = []
# Keep every dict entry, id or not. Filtering on the id first made a malformed
# refusal disappear from BOTH the count and the table - a fixer that emitted an
# id-less refusal got a green gate, which is failing open on bad output.
ledger = [e for e in ledger if isinstance(e, dict)]

pushed = flag('PUSHED')
clean = flag('CLEAN')
verify_ok = flag('VERIFY_OK')
# verify_run reports passed=true when it SKIPPED (no verify.sh was produced).
# Tolerable as a loop signal; on a required check, claiming a green build that
# never ran is a false statement, so a skip blocks like a red one.
verify_skipped = flag('VERIFY_SKIPPED')

# Nothing was pushed and no review was answered: this run has nothing to say
# about this head, and whatever verdict already sits on it is still the truth.
# Overwriting it would be speaking for a revision this run did not change.
if not pushed and not ledger:
    emit(False, 'nothing pushed and no prior review answered - left the existing verdict alone')

unresolved = [e for e in ledger if str(e.get('status') or '').strip().lower() != 'fixed']
fixed = [e for e in ledger if str(e.get('status') or '').strip().lower() == 'fixed']
# A fix that did not land is not a fix. push_back_tool reports pushed=false for a
# protected branch, a non-fast-forward, a rebase conflict, a dead token - and the
# head then still carries every finding the ledger calls fixed.
fixes_stranded = bool(fixed) and not pushed

body = ['## Branch review by the improvement loop (iterion)', '']
if pushed:
    body.append('Pushed fixes onto this branch. ' + str(os.environ.get('PUSH_REASON') or '').strip())
else:
    body.append('No commits pushed. ' + str(os.environ.get('PUSH_REASON') or '').strip())
summary = str(os.environ.get('SUMMARY') or '').strip()
if summary:
    body.append('')
    body.append(summary)

if ledger:
    body.append('')
    body.append('### What became of the previous review')
    body.append('')
    body.append('| finding | outcome | note |')
    body.append('| --- | --- | --- |')
    for e in ledger:
        note = str(e.get('note') or '').replace(chr(124), '/').replace(nl, ' ')
        outcome = str(e.get('status') or '').strip().lower() or 'unknown'
        commit = str(e.get('commit') or '').strip()
        if commit:
            outcome = outcome + ' (' + commit[:12] + ')'
        body.append('| ' + str(e.get('id')) + ' | ' + outcome + ' | ' + note[:300] + ' |')
    if unresolved:
        body.append('')
        body.append('A finding that was not fixed keeps the merge gate red, whether it was contested or deferred. A maintainer arbitrates it - by pushing a fix, or by approving the finding away.')

gate_enabled = flag('GATE_ENABLED')
gate_ctx = str(os.environ.get('GATE_CONTEXT') or '').strip()
payload = {'pr_url': pr, 'summary': nl.join(body), 'mode': 'summary', 'comments': []}
if gate_enabled and gate_ctx:
    blocking = len(unresolved)
    if fixes_stranded:
        blocking += len(fixed)
    if not clean:
        blocking += 1
    if not verify_ok or verify_skipped:
        blocking += 1
    if blocking == 0 and not ledger:
        # Nothing reviewed this head, so there is no review to report as clean.
        blocking = 1
        note = 'code pushed but no review of this revision - awaiting one'
    elif blocking == 0:
        note = str(len(ledger)) + ' finding(s) fixed, re-review by the fixer clean, build green'
    else:
        bits = []
        if unresolved:
            bits.append(str(len(unresolved)) + ' finding(s) unresolved')
        if fixes_stranded:
            bits.append(str(len(fixed)) + ' fix(es) never reached this head')
        if not clean:
            bits.append('issues remain in the diff')
        if not verify_ok:
            bits.append('build/tests red')
        elif verify_skipped:
            bits.append('build/tests never ran')
        note = ', '.join(bits) + ' - not mergeable yet'
    payload['gate'] = {'enabled': True, 'context': gate_ctx, 'blocking_count': blocking,
                       'threshold': 'ledger', 'total_findings': len(ledger), 'note': note}

req = urllib.request.Request(url, data=json.dumps(payload).encode(),
                             headers={'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'X-Iterion-Run': tok},
                             method='POST')
try:
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=120) as resp:
        res = json.loads(resp.read().decode())
except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:
    try:
        detail = e.read().decode()[:500].strip()
    except Exception:
        detail = ''
    emit(False, 'forge publish endpoint HTTP ' + str(e.code) + ': ' + detail)
except Exception as e:
    emit(False, 'forge publish endpoint unreachable: ' + str(e)[:200])

note = 'verdict posted'
if res.get('gate_posted'):
    note = note + '; ' + str(res.get('gate_context') or '') + '=' + str(res.get('gate_state') or '') + ' on ' + str(res.get('gate_sha') or '')[:12]
elif res.get('gate_error'):
    note = note + '; gate NOT posted: ' + str(res.get('gate_error'))[:200]
emit(True, note)
"`
  output: post_feedback_output

## ─── Workflow ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

workflow branch_improve_loop:
  ## Entry is the deterministic workspace precondition: a launch with no
  ## repository to work on, or whose base_ref is not reachable from HEAD,
  ## fails typed (WORKSPACE_NOT_A_REPO) before any LLM node spends. Then
  ## the plan phase: on by default, the diff triage is AUTHORED (a
  ## read-only map, not the v1 blocking diff-chunk scan), peer-reviewed
  ## cross-model ONLY when `plan_review` resolved on at launch (a second
  ## model family credentialed), bounded by plan_budget_gate, and handed
  ## to the campaign — stamped with its provenance — before it fixes.
  ## `plan_phase: off` starts the campaign immediately (plan in stride).
  ## A re-dispatch's campaign reads `git log`, re-diffs the branch, and
  ## continues either way.
  entry: workspace_probe

  ## Run in an isolated git worktree at .iterion/worktrees/<run-id>/. The bot
  ## mutates code AND commits; the worktree + finalize FF guarantees: (a) the
  ## operator's main checkout is untouched during the run, (b) on convergence
  ## the commits land on a persistent branch `iterion/run/<friendly-name>` AND
  ## fast-forward the operator's currently-checked-out branch (best-effort,
  ## skipped with a warning on conflict).
  worktree: auto

  ## No `sandbox:` block (ADR-082): the platform sandboxes by default
  ## (`sandbox: auto` — target repo's devcontainer, else the default
  ## image). The bot's own tools (gh/glab for the PR-comment tail) come
  ## from the sibling devbox.json; the target repo's toolchain from its
  ## own devcontainer/devbox.json.

  budget:
    max_parallel_branches: 1
    ## +30m/+$15 headroom for the opt-in plan phase (ADR-091): when
    ## plan_review resolves on, ~3 LLM calls run before the campaign.
    max_duration: "2h30m"
    max_cost_usd: 75

  compaction:
    threshold: 0.9
    preserve_recent: 8

  ## Precondition: nothing LLM runs on a workspace with no repository
  ## (or with an unreachable base_ref).
  workspace_probe -> plan_topology when ok
  workspace_probe -> workspace_not_a_repo when not ok

  ## Plan phase: on by default; plan_phase off → straight to the campaign.
  plan_topology -> plan_scope_probe when do_plan
  ## Every campaign_input field is mapped EXPLICITLY empty: an unmapped
  ## field is not "" but the raw {{input.x}} placeholder leaking into
  ## the prompt (the engine keeps unresolved refs as-is).
  plan_topology -> campaign when not do_plan with {
    fail_log: "", plan: "", plan_critique: "", plan_responses: "",
    plan_provenance: ""
  }

  plan_scope_probe -> plan with {
    diff_stat: "{{outputs.plan_scope_probe.diff_stat}}",
    large:     "{{outputs.plan_scope_probe.large}}"
  }
  ## The authored triage meets the peer-review gate: a second model
  ## family credentialed (plan_review on) → cross-model critique + author
  ## revision; otherwise the triage goes straight to the budget gate
  ## unreviewed, with a provenance stamp that says so.
  plan -> plan_review_topology
  plan_review_topology -> plan_review when do_review with {
    plan:        "{{outputs.plan.plan}}",
    assumptions: "{{outputs.plan.assumptions}}",
    risks:       "{{outputs.plan.risks}}",
    diff_stat:   "{{outputs.plan_scope_probe.diff_stat}}",
    large:       "{{outputs.plan_scope_probe.large}}"
  }
  plan_review_topology -> plan_budget_gate when not do_review with {
    plan: "{{outputs.plan.plan}}",
    plan_critique: "", plan_responses: "",
    plan_provenance: "authored by the plan node - NOT peer-reviewed (plan_review resolved off: one model family credentialed, or the operator said off); the author-written triage, unchallenged"
  }
  plan_review -> plan_gate
  ## Peer served AND the diff is small enough to be worth a second read →
  ## the plan AUTHOR (same session) challenges the critique and integrates
  ## what holds. skip_revise (peer unavailable OR large diff, native:695)
  ## → the plan/critique pass straight to the budget gate unrevised.
  plan_gate -> plan_revise when not skip_revise with {
    plan:        "{{outputs.plan.plan}}",
    assumptions: "{{outputs.plan.assumptions}}",
    risks:       "{{outputs.plan.risks}}",
    concerns:    "{{outputs.plan_review.concerns}}",
    suggestions: "{{outputs.plan_review.suggestions}}",
    blocking:    "{{outputs.plan_review.blocking}}",
    _session_id: "{{outputs.plan._session_id}}",
    _session_fingerprint: "{{outputs.plan._session_fingerprint}}"
  }
  plan_gate -> plan_budget_gate when skip_revise with {
    plan: "{{outputs.plan.plan}}",
    plan_critique: "{{outputs.plan_review.concerns}}",
    plan_responses: "",
    plan_provenance: "{{outputs.plan_gate.provenance}}"
  }
  plan_revise -> plan_budget_gate with {
    plan:            "{{outputs.plan_revise.plan}}",
    plan_critique:   "{{outputs.plan_review.concerns}}",
    plan_responses:  "{{outputs.plan_revise.review_responses}}",
    plan_provenance: "authored by the plan node, critiqued by a cross-model peer, and revised by the author (its accepted/rejected responses follow)"
  }

  ## Within its share → the campaign starts, carrying the (possibly
  ## unrevised) plan forward exactly as the pre-guard edges did. Over its
  ## share → `plan_exhausted`, which stamps PLAN_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED and the
  ## comparison on the RUN (failure_code / error, so `iterion runs list`,
  ## the studio and the alert sinks read it) instead of spending
  ## campaign's reserve (native:695). The refusal is resumable: widen the
  ## caps, resume, and this guard is re-evaluated.
  plan_budget_gate -> campaign when not exhausted with {
    fail_log:        "",
    plan:            "{{outputs.plan_budget_gate.plan}}",
    plan_critique:   "{{outputs.plan_budget_gate.plan_critique}}",
    plan_responses:  "{{outputs.plan_budget_gate.plan_responses}}",
    plan_provenance: "{{outputs.plan_budget_gate.plan_provenance}}"
  }
  plan_budget_gate -> plan_exhausted when exhausted

  ## Each pass: the campaign reviews+improves the branch diff (committing each
  ## fix in stride), then the deterministic build/test gate re-checks the tree.
  campaign -> verify_probe with {
    iteration: "{{loop.continuation_loop.iteration}}"
  }
  ## verify_probe reuses an already-valid verify.sh (skipping the LLM
  ## verify_build) on passes 2+; the first pass of every run regenerates it for
  ## the current tree. Both outgoing edges test the bool `fresh` (C012-exhaustive).
  verify_probe -> verify_run when fresh
  verify_probe -> verify_build when not fresh
  verify_build -> verify_run
  ## In-loop adversarial review of the branch diff before the gate decides.
  verify_run -> review with {
    base_ref:       "{{vars.base_ref}}",
    build_passed:   "{{outputs.verify_run.passed}}",
    prior_findings: "{{outputs.gate.fail_log}}"
  }
  review -> gate

  ## Converged (green AND the campaign reports the branch diff clean) → ship
  ## what is banked (optional PR, else finish).
  gate -> mr_gate when converged
  ## Not converged → another campaign pass. RED carries the failure log so the
  ## agent fixes what it broke; green-but-more-work carries an empty log so the
  ## agent simply keeps reviewing the diff. This closes the single declared
  ## loop (campaign→verify_build→verify_run→gate→campaign); the loop-back reads
  ## outputs.gate.fail_log (a BODY node — never the loop-head campaign's own
  ## output on its back-edge, which would read a loop-entry-frozen value).
  ## The back-edge BLANKS the plan-phase fields: forward-edge mappings
  ## re-apply on every re-entry (outputs persist), and a stale pass-1
  ## plan would re-anchor later passes that must read `git log` instead.
  gate -> campaign as continuation_loop("{{vars.max_passes}}") with {
    fail_log: "{{outputs.gate.fail_log}}",
    plan: "", plan_critique: "", plan_responses: "", plan_provenance: ""
  }
  ## continuation_loop exhausted (max_passes hit while issues still remain) →
  ## ship what is banked anyway (every committed fix is real, landed work).
  ## Unconditional fallthrough after the loop-back retries are spent.
  gate -> mr_gate

  ## Converged (or capped) → exactly one of: PR path, PR push-back path, or
  ## finish (mr_gate's three booleans are mutually exclusive). Each remote
  ## path first pays a ~100ms deterministic credential probe before any
  ## agent/push work.
  mr_gate -> forge_auth_probe when open_mr
  mr_gate -> push_auth_probe when push_back
  mr_gate -> done

  ## PR-context push-back: credential present → deterministically push the
  ## run's HEAD onto the PR's source branch (push_back_tool no-ops when
  ## rev-list shows nothing new). Absent → finish; the commits stay on the
  ## engine's storage branch (ephemeral on cloud runners — the probe result
  ## says why in the run report).
  push_auth_probe -> push_back_tool when available
  push_auth_probe -> done when not available
  ## After the deterministic push (pushed or clean no-op), Billy posts his
  ## review verdict as a comment ON the PR so the author reads the conclusion
  ## in the forge, carrying the campaign summary + the push result.
  push_back_tool -> publish_verdict with {
    pr_url:         "{{vars.pr_url}}",
    review_summary: "{{outputs.campaign.summary}}",
    branch_clean:   "{{outputs.campaign.branch_clean}}",
    commits_pushed: "{{outputs.push_back_tool.pushed}}",
    push_reason:    "{{outputs.push_back_tool.reason}}",
    finding_ledger: "{{outputs.campaign.finding_ledger}}",
    verify_ok:      "{{outputs.gate.converged}}",
    verify_skipped: "{{outputs.verify_run.skipped}}"
  }
  publish_verdict -> done

  ## Push credential present → finalize_mr pushes the run's HEAD (the series of
  ## per-pass commits) + opens the PR and (when source_issue_ref is set)
  ## posts the URL back. Absent → finish cleanly; the commits are on the
  ## engine's persistent iterion/run/<name> storage branch for a manual push.
  forge_auth_probe -> finalize_mr when available with {
    workspace_dir:    "{{vars.workspace_dir}}",
    mr_branch:        "{{vars.mr_branch}}",
    mr_base:          "{{vars.mr_base}}",
    source_issue_ref: "{{vars.source_issue_ref}}",
    scope_notes:      "{{vars.scope_notes}}"
  }
  forge_auth_probe -> done when not available
  ## PR opened → surface its URL as a headline result-link in the run summary
  ## (deterministic stdout directive → preview_url_available kind=pr), then
  ## finish. Nothing opened (empty diff / refused PR) → straight to done.
  finalize_mr -> surface_pr_link when opened with { url: "{{outputs.finalize_mr.url}}" }
  finalize_mr -> done when not opened
  surface_pr_link -> done
